## ADR-010: Highlights via text-node wrapping with TreeWalker + one regex

**Status:** Accepted

**Context.** Saved words must be highlighted by mastery level without breaking the host
page (layout, scripts, event handlers, selections).

**Alternatives considered.**
- `innerHTML` rewriting: fast but destroys event handlers and re-parses markup — rejected.
- CSS Custom Highlight API (`::highlight`): elegant and non-mutating, but per-range styling
  by mastery level and hover tooltips are limited; wrapping gives both for free.

**Decision.**
- One regex built from all saved words, longest-first, with Unicode word boundaries
  (`(?<![\p{L}\p{M}])…(?![\p{L}\p{M}])`), case-insensitive.
- TreeWalker collects text nodes, skipping `script/style/code/pre/contenteditable/iframe`
  and our own spans; matches are wrapped in `span.wc-hl.wc-m{0..6}` with a `title` tooltip.
- `MutationObserver` rescans only newly added subtrees (infinite scroll, SPAs), debounced
  250 ms, and explicitly ignores self-caused mutations (no feedback loop).
- `storage.onChanged` (debounced 300 ms) triggers unwrap + rescan, so grading in the
  review tab live-recolors any open reading tab.

**Consequences.**
- (+) Non-destructive; dblclick capture still works on highlighted words; the tooltip
  "Already in your list" path naturally covers highlighted words.
- (+) Highlight classes are pure CSS — color scheme changes without touching JS.
- (−) Exact match only: inflections (`fui` vs `ir`) and plurals don't match. Stemming
  requires per-language support — deferred.
- (−) Shadow DOM subtrees and cross-origin iframes are not scanned (`all_frames: true`
  would cover same-origin iframes; Shadow DOM would need per-root attach).
- (−) DOM size grows slightly on word-dense pages; acceptable.

